{"type": "assistant", "message": {"model": "claude-sonnet-5-5", "id": "msg_011CfbaeNWwQzakvPAeg1D62", "type": "message", "role": "assistant", "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "Releases are cut by pushing a git tag named `vYYYY.MM.N`, for example `v2026.09.2`. CI then builds and publishes. Never publish by hand. This comes from a stored project convention in EngramDB.\n\nThe steps:\n\n1. Pick the next tag. `N` is the release counter within that month. `pyproject.toml` currently says `version = \"2026.9.1\"`, so the next September release would be `v2026.09.2`. October releases would start at `v2026.10.1`. I'm assuming `N` starts at 1 each month, since the memory only gives one example.\n2. Make sure `main` is clean and the tests pass. The repo has a convention to run them with `make test-fast`.\n3. Create and push the tag, for example `git tag v2026.10.1 && git push origin v2026.10.1`.\n4. Let CI build and publish.\n\nI found nothing else about releases in the repo: no CI workflow files, no changelog, no release doc, and no release or publish target in the `Makefile`. So I can't confirm what CI does after the tag is pushed. I also can't tell whether you're meant to bump `pyproject.toml` before tagging. The version string there uses `2026.9.1` without a zero pad, while the tag format uses `2026.09.2`.\n\nI haven't tagged or pushed anything."}], "container": null, "stop_reason": null, "stop_sequence": null, "stop_details": null, "usage": {"input_tokens": 2, "cache_creation_input_tokens": 259, "cache_read_input_tokens": 22319, "cache_creation": {"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens": 0, "ephemeral_1h_input_tokens": 259}, "output_tokens": 431, "service_tier": "standard", "inference_geo": "not_available"}, "input_transformations": [], "diagnostics": null, "context_management": null}, "parent_tool_use_id": null, "session_id": "255f5c03-ab5e-49d6-bdfc-9377bad55080", "uuid": "b53e50a3-9026-40fe-9b80-164876c7ba79", "timestamp": "2026-10-01T13:16:52.757Z", "request_id": "req_011Cfba